"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3316],{33316:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"02-audit","title":"Lesson 02. Spending audit: Where money leaks away","sourceFingerprint":"75a2b6cd17712e1c99987c375cd5c74f6323e10b1c82c9526db4c4e39bfe03dd","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"## Spending audit"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"Where money leaks away"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"Ask friends how much they spend monthly and most answer immediately. Then ask them to open their banking apps and add actual spending for 3 months. The real figure is almost always higher, sometimes 1.5 times as much. They are not lying. The brain remembers large purchases and overlooks small ones, yet those small ones consume the budget."},{"id":"b004","markdown":"Food works similarly: until you record it, you seem to eat little. Then biscuits with tea, a snack in the car and “a couple of bites” in the evening turn out to make up half a daily allowance. In this lesson we record what really happens to your money. It takes one evening and costs nothing."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"### Step 1. Collect all spending for 3 months"},{"id":"b006","markdown":"One month can easily be unusual: a holiday, birthday or car repair. But 3 months provide a fairer average. Sources to use:"},{"id":"b007","markdown":"- Banking apps. Almost all offer category analysis. Export 3 months of transactions from every bank where you hold a card."},{"id":"b008","markdown":"- Cash. Honestly estimate how much you withdraw from cash machines monthly."},{"id":"b009","markdown":"- Marketplaces. A significant portion of spending often hides here; check your order history."},{"id":"b010","markdown":"- Subscriptions. Check charges from the App Store, Google Play, your mobile operator and the bank itself."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"Combine everything in a single table in Excel, Google Sheets or even notes. What matters is having it all in one place."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"Important: exclude transfers between your own accounts. They simply move money from one pocket to another."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"### Step 2. Sort spending into 3 baskets"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"You do not need 40 categories. The bank’s analytics already did that, with limited benefit. Just 3 baskets are enough."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"#### Basket 1. Mandatory"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"Things without which life stops or becomes substantially worse: housing, including rent or mortgage and utilities; basic food; commuting; communications; required loan payments; and medicine."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"#### Basket 2. Lifestyle"},{"id":"b018","markdown":"Things you choose that make life pleasant: cafes, travel, hobbies and sport, clothing beyond essentials and gifts. This is what people earn for; there is nothing wrong with it."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"#### Basket 3. Leaks"},{"id":"b020","markdown":"Imagine a dripping tap. A drop a second seems trivial, but over a month it adds several hundred litres to the water bill. Money leaks work the same way. You did not deliberately choose these expenses; they somehow happened:"},{"id":"b021","markdown":"- Subscriptions you do not use."},{"id":"b022","markdown":"- Delivery when cooking felt like effort even though there was food at home."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"- Bank fees and late-payment penalties."},{"id":"b024","markdown":"- Impulse marketplace purchases that remain unopened."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"- Paid app and game features bought once and forgotten."},{"id":"b026","markdown":"- Credit-card interest."},{"id":"b027","markdown":"Leaks are your first source of money for savings."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"THREE BASKETS. A DROP A SECOND, HUNDREDS OF LITRES A MONTH. MANDATORY. LIFESTYLE. LEAKS: START HERE. Sort your spending into baskets."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"Apartment rent."},{"id":"b030","markdown":"Mandatory. Lifestyle. Leak."},{"id":"b031","markdown":"### Step 3. Calculate the shares"},{"id":"b032","markdown":"Divide each basket’s total by all spending over 3 months. There are no universally correct percentages. Someone with a Moscow mortgage and someone living in a parent’s apartment in Kazan will have very different shares. But notice 2 signals. Leaks above 10% of total spending mean money for saving already exists; it is simply going elsewhere."},{"id":"b033","markdown":"Mandatory spending above 70% means there is little left to cut. The main lever is income rather than savings; remember lesson 1. If both warnings apply, start with leaks because that is faster."},{"id":"b034","markdown":"### What is worth the money"},{"id":"b035","markdown":"After an audit, people often ask what it is reasonable to spend on. There is no universal list, but 4 questions help."},{"id":"b036","markdown":"#### How many hours of life does it cost?"},{"id":"b037","markdown":"In lesson 1 we calculated an hour’s price, about 570 RUB at average pay. A 12 000 RUB item costs roughly 21 working hours, almost 3 working days. Sometimes it is worth it; often it is not."},{"id":"b038","markdown":"#### Does it save time or health, or generate income?"},{"id":"b039","markdown":"Some expenses pay back: good shoes if you walk a lot; a course that supports asking for a raise; a dishwasher that frees 5 hours weekly; dentistry now rather than a year later when treatment costs several times more. Cutting these expenses usually costs more."},{"id":"b040","markdown":"#### In a month, will I remember why I bought it?"},{"id":"b041","markdown":"A useful lifestyle-basket test. You will remember a trip with friends for years, while forgetting a fifth discounted T-shirt within a week."},{"id":"b042","markdown":"#### Is it for me or to be seen by someone else?"},{"id":"b043","markdown":"Status purchases are the most expensive and lose their value fastest. A car on credit so you do not feel embarrassed arriving at a meeting. A 300-thousand-ruble laptop used for email and television. A watch intended to catch colleagues’ attention. You do not have to reject them, but honestly ask who commissioned the purchase."},{"id":"b044","markdown":"### The 72-hour rule"},{"id":"b045","markdown":"A simple defence against impulse buying. Want an unplanned purchase costing more than a few working hours? Put it in the basket or write it down, then return after 72 hours. If you still want it, buy calmly. The state has adopted a similar logic for loans. Since September 1, 2025, most consumer-loan money is released after 4 or 48 hours depending on amount, rather than immediately. More in lesson 4."},{"id":"b046","markdown":"### What not to do"},{"id":"b047","markdown":"Cut everything at once. Severe austerity breaks down after a couple of months. Remove leaks and initially leave lifestyle spending alone. Eliminate all pleasures. Keep an amount, however small, to spend freely without accounting for it; this protects against lapses. Turn tracking into a job. You need not record every purchase for years: repeat this audit every 3–6 months."},{"id":"b048","markdown":"### The essentials"},{"id":"b049","markdown":"- Almost everyone underestimates spending. An audit across 3 months reveals the real picture."},{"id":"b050","markdown":"- Just 3 baskets are enough: mandatory, lifestyle and leaks. Leaks are the easiest starting point."},{"id":"b051","markdown":"- Before buying, translate the price into working hours and ask whether it returns time, health or income."},{"id":"b052","markdown":"Next lesson we turn the money found into a system that does not depend on willpower."}],"terms":[],"quizzes":[{"id":"02-audit-q1","q":"Why audit 3 months rather than 1?","options":["Banks only keep 3 months of history","One month can be unusual, while three provide a fairer average","The tax authority requires it"],"correct":1},{"id":"02-audit-q2","q":"Which of these is a leak?","options":["Apartment rent","A holiday you planned for half a year","A subscription unused for 3 months"],"correct":2},{"id":"02-audit-q3","q":"Mandatory expenses are 80% of the budget and there are almost no leaks. What is the main lever?","options":["Increasing income","Giving up food","A new credit card"],"correct":0}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);